ons of nitrogen during  this five minute presentation 1100  tons of new nitrogen will be produced  furthermore haber-bosch nitrogen is  extremely inefficient on average half of  this nitrogen is lost to the biosphere  leading to eutrophication and greenhouse  gas emissions it's likely that we've  already altered the global nitrogen  cycle to a point where the extra  nitrogen is driving irreversible changes  in the biosphere beyond these  environmental concerns new knowledge  about the role of the plant microbiome  the microbial communities associated  with plants points the way towards  sustainable nutrient management  strategies plants and their micro biomes  have been co-evolving for seven hundred  million years and greater understanding  of the role of the plant microbiome has  overturned our view of plant nutrient  acquisition and biological soil  processes decomposition is a case in  point until recently decomposition has  been viewed as a microbially governed  process occurring in bulk soil  independent of plants a view compatible  with the strategy of delivering  available nitrogen directly to crops  it's now clear that a continuum of plant  microbial associations govern  decomposition plants can stimulate  decomposition by providing carbon to  decomposers in exchange for nitrogen and  other nutrients the carbon provides  energy to subsidize the cost of  expensive XO enzymes decomposers must  make to free nitrogen this exchange of  carbon for nutrients in the rhizosphere  provides an efficient means for  delivering nitrogen to plants so much so  that even under optimal fertilizer  management regimes crops still rely  heavily on soil organic nitrogen pools  reflecting their coevolution with  decomposers for example and  conventionally managed grains receiving  recommended fertilizer rates fertilizer  nitrogen accounts for less than forty  percent of the night  taken up by the crops to promote  sustainable nitrogen management we need  to use systems thinking th